<p>Wintering Grounds &#x2013; <a href="https://winteringgrounds.bandcamp.com/album/impasse?from=search&amp;search_item_id=1638995777&amp;search_item_type=a&amp;search_match_part=%3F&amp;search_page_id=5734362326&amp;search_page_no=1&amp;search_rank=1&amp;search_sig=3a3996e5e1944155c356db91ef417b9d&amp;ref=heavyblogisheavy.com">Impasse</a> (progressive death metal)</p><p>Worldsucks &#x2013; <a href="https://worldsucks.bandcamp.com/album/scorn-for-the-u-s-a?from=search&amp;search_item_id=611836009&amp;search_item_type=a&amp;search_match_part=%3F&amp;search_page_id=5734362487&amp;search_page_no=1&amp;search_rank=1&amp;search_sig=2fb4432a88dbf10fe367a5d7ddaca506&amp;ref=heavyblogisheavy.com">Scorn for the U.S.A.</a> (blackened grind)</p><p>Xion &#x2013; <a href="https://ff-xion.bandcamp.com/album/when-stars-collide?from=search&amp;search_item_id=3944243339&amp;search_item_type=a&amp;search_match_part=%3F&amp;search_page_id=5734362040&amp;search_page_no=1&amp;search_rank=1&amp;search_sig=745d1b4729c92a5b31b26341eb137b18&amp;ref=heavyblogisheavy.com">When Stars Collide</a> (heavy metal, thrash)</p>";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}}}i:1;a:6:{s:4:"data";s:0:"";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";s:5:"child";a:4:{s:0:"";a:6:{s:5:"title";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:25:"Green Lung - Necropolitan";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:11:"description";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:225:"what's on this album is very good - no one will catch me skipping a Green Lung track when I want to rock the fuck out. But sometimes I want other things and Green Lung's previous albums gave that to me. Necropolitan does not.";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:4:"link";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:68:"https://www.heavyblogisheavy.com/2026/09/10/green-lung-necropolitan/";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:4:"guid";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:24:"6aa20da5d3263100011dcbc3";s:7:"attribs";a:1:{s:0:"";a:1:{s:11:"isPermaLink";s:5:"false";}}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:8:"category";a:2:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:7:"Reviews";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}i:1;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:10:"Green Lung";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:7:"pubDate";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:29:"Thu, 10 Sep 2026 14:00:19 GMT";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:32:"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/";a:1:{s:7:"creator";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:15:"Eden Kupermintz";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:29:"http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/";a:1:{s:7:"content";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:0:"";s:7:"attribs";a:1:{s:0:"";a:2:{s:3:"url";s:103:"https://storage.ghost.io/c/8b/c6/8bc603be-b831-44f6-8ffb-773be5bd45c8/content/images/2026/09/422469.jpg";s:6:"medium";s:5:"image";}}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:40:"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/";a:1:{s:7:"encoded";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:3681:"<img src="https://storage.ghost.io/c/8b/c6/8bc603be-b831-44f6-8ffb-773be5bd45c8/content/images/2026/09/422469.jpg" alt="Green Lung - Necropolitan"><p>If you ask me (and you are, since you&apos;re reading this review), the power of <strong>Green Lung</strong> always lay in the tension between their rock n&apos; roll groove and their power ballad delivery. For me, neither part was strong enough to stand on its own; rather, the way each set up the other was what made Green Lung so good. Moving from &quot;Hunters in the Sky&quot; into &quot;Oceans of Time&quot; for example (both from <em>This Heathen Land</em>) just gets me every time. The former is ripping and roaring track arranged around a monstrously infectious chorus while the latter is a gothic, bombastic, and flamboyant tribute to one of the best divas to ever do it, Count Vlad Dracula himself. It&apos;s the tension and the payoff that keeps me coming back again and again to the album and, indeed, to Green Lung&apos;s entire backlog.</p><p>Maybe you disagree; maybe you love Green Lung simply for their ability to conjure the spirit of rock n&apos; roll at their tip of their guitars and infuse it with <em>just</em> enough stoner metal magic. I know at least one person like this and while I disagree, I respect the position; there is no doubt that Green Lung rock. If that&apos;s what you&apos;re after, then <em>Necropolitan</em>, their upcoming release, has it in spades. From the big main riff of &quot;Black Magic Radio&quot; to the soaring solo and vocals of &quot;Together in Death&quot; (some of the best from the band on both accounts), <em>Necropolitan </em>is all rock. There&apos;s a touch of the power ballad on tracks like &quot;To the Gallows Born&quot;, which closes the album, and even some at the beginning of the aforementioned &quot;Together in Death&quot;. But that&apos;s about it; the rest is dedicated to the worship of the riff.</p><p>And, for me, this is also where the album&apos;s weakness lies. I found myself getting a bit lost in the sea of licks, grooves, and ear-worm targeted choruses. When I tuned in, everything I heard was good, even very good. But it was hard to tune in, as the mode of the album maintained the same pace, the same vibe, and the same intent. I longed for the contrast; the first two tracks went by and I headbanged and danced and had fun but then I wanted to be a bit wistful, you know? To raise my arms in praise of a higher power. To dream of the trees. Green Lung stuff, you know? And it was completely absent. </p><p>There are so many examples from Green Lung&apos;s career that I could add to the ones in the first paragraph - &quot;May Queen&quot;, &quot;One For Sorrow&quot;, &quot;Graveyard Sun&quot;. These are some of Green Lung&apos;s best and most memorable tracks, exactly because they temper the hot, sharp steel of Green Lung&apos;s groove with a colder, more epic, and more grandiose iron that, in my opinion, is sorely needed. However, as I said, what&apos;s on this album is very good - no one will catch me skipping a Green Lung track when the window is down and the car is going fast and I want to rock the fuck out. But sometimes I want other things and Green Lung&apos;s previous albums gave that to me. <em>Necropolitan</em> does not. Alas!</p>
<!--kg-card-begin: html-->
