<p>Vredesverk &#x2013; <a href="https://vredesverk.bandcamp.com/album/the-sanguine-heir?from=search&amp;search_item_id=3076358358&amp;search_item_type=a&amp;search_match_part=%3F&amp;search_page_id=5710748064&amp;search_page_no=1&amp;search_rank=1&amp;search_sig=20053fceb9f5d28936ae6096db938af1&amp;ref=heavyblogisheavy.com">The Sanguine Heir</a> (symphonic melodeath)</p><p>Wince and Frisson &#x2013; <a href="https://winceandfrisson.bandcamp.com/album/tumult?from=search&amp;search_item_id=897706554&amp;search_item_type=a&amp;search_match_part=%3F&amp;search_page_id=5710748167&amp;search_page_no=1&amp;search_rank=1&amp;search_sig=10139f6682b48c7e5defe01d9853b095&amp;ref=heavyblogisheavy.com">Tumult</a> (blackened death metal)</p><p>Witchburner &#x2013; <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=vr6O2xD1wkg&amp;ref=heavyblogisheavy.com">Black Raging Flames</a> (thrash)</p><p><a>Zahna &#x2013; </a><a href="https://youtu.be/rOJvMpNkUZE?ref=heavyblogisheavy.com">Break Or Breakthrough</a> (pop metal, baddiecore)</p><p><a>Zvindiyar &#x2013; </a><a href="https://zvindiyar.bandcamp.com/album/six-rites?ref=heavyblogisheavy.com">Six Rites</a> (progressive death metal)</p>";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}}}i:5;a:6:{s:4:"data";s:0:"";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";s:5:"child";a:4:{s:0:"";a:6:{s:5:"title";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:44:"Showing Teeth - A Fate Worse Than Loneliness";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:11:"description";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:138:"This is an incredibly promising debut release showcasing a serious vocal talent that will likely be filling arenas in years to come.   ";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:4:"link";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:87:"https://www.heavyblogisheavy.com/2026/09/03/showing-teeth-a-fate-worse-than-loneliness/";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:4:"guid";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:24:"6a98aa3540c7b200013aaf93";s:7:"attribs";a:1:{s:0:"";a:1:{s:11:"isPermaLink";s:5:"false";}}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:8:"category";a:2:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:7:"Reviews";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}i:1;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:13:"Showing Teeth";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:7:"pubDate";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:29:"Thu, 03 Sep 2026 16:00:03 GMT";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:32:"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/";a:1:{s:7:"creator";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:10:"Phil Knock";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:29:"http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/";a:1:{s:7:"content";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:0:"";s:7:"attribs";a:1:{s:0:"";a:2:{s:3:"url";s:103:"https://storage.ghost.io/c/8b/c6/8bc603be-b831-44f6-8ffb-773be5bd45c8/content/images/2026/09/128126.png";s:6:"medium";s:5:"image";}}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:40:"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/";a:1:{s:7:"encoded";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:3966:"<img src="https://storage.ghost.io/c/8b/c6/8bc603be-b831-44f6-8ffb-773be5bd45c8/content/images/2026/09/128126.png" alt="Showing Teeth - A Fate Worse Than Loneliness"><p>There is a considerable buzz around <strong>Showing<em> </em>Teeth</strong> with the project already collecting nearly a quarter of a million followers on Instagram. Throw in a recent cover interview with <em>Kerrang! </em>plus a support slot for <strong>Bring Me The Horizon</strong> at their huge show in Manchester&apos;s BEC arena, and it&apos;s fair to say expectations are high. The band is essentially a solo project (there is a band for live shows), with versatile singer/songwriter Addison being the sole creative mind behind everything on debut EP, <em>A Fate Worse Than Loneliness.</em></p><p>This may be Showing Teeth&apos;s debut EP, but the quality of production would tell you otherwise. Everything feels polished, driven and agonised over. However, as is often the case on debut releases, it is difficult to pin down a definitive sound, mainly due to the songwriting dipping toes into so many genres throughout the seven tracks on offer. Initially you&apos;re lured into thinking this is going to be a blast of frenetic death/metalcore, with opener &#x201C;Bad Exchanges&#x201D; blowing the doors clean off with its bludgeoning intensity. Think along the lines of <strong>Knocked Loose</strong> teaming up with <strong>Employed To Serve </strong>at their<strong> </strong>heaviest and you&#x2019;re getting close. But, by the time you get to the soaring <strong>Ariana Grande</strong>-esque pop choruses on finale &#x201C;Paradigm&#x201D;, you might be a tad confused with what direction this project wants to go in.&#xA0;&#xA0;</p><p>Addison&apos;s vocal range is absolutely incredible and clearly the star of the show. Even before we get to the clean vocals her palette of screams are simply stunning. Whether it be guttural growls or high pitched rasps it&apos;s all delivered with energy and venom. I&#x2019;m pleased the clean vocals are not implemented lavishly on every track, especially as there is so much variation in the screams. For example, there&apos;s plenty of spiky texture on &#x201C;Labyrinth&#x201D; without the, admittedly catchy as hell, angelic chorus. Djent-laden &#x201C;Rip&#x201D; uses them in a different way, with a fleeting cameo at the one minute mark, before returning for a suitably flourishing finale. &#x201C;Make It Out&#x201D; and &#x201C;Shallow Grave&#x201D; do feel like slight mis-steps and go too far down a predictable nu-metal path, but I&#x2019;m sure I will be in the minority on this point as the more accessible vocals are definitely a huge part of the bands appeal. In fact, I&#x2019;m going to completely contradict myself now, but I would have preferred &#x201C;Paradigm&#x201D; to be brave, ditch the screams entirely, and just embrace its pop anthem glory. </p><p>It will be interesting to see which direction they decide to go in for their inevitable full length release, and I hope they manage to laser focus their sound while still holding on to the ferocity present on &quot;Bad Exchanges&quot;. I have no doubt that Showing Teeth are going to blow up insanely big over the next 12-18 months and this can only be a good thing for extreme metal, especially if they act as a new gateway for kids to discover other heavy bands. That being said, this is an incredibly promising debut release showcasing a serious vocal talent that will likely be filling arenas in years to come.&#xA0;&#xA0;&#xA0;</p><figure class="kg-card kg-embed-card"><iframe width="200" height="150" src="https://www.youtube.com/embed/el2qML9p1-s?feature=oembed" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" referrerpolicy="strict-origin-when-cross-origin" allowfullscreen title="Showing Teeth - Bad Exchanges"></iframe></figure><p>&#xA0;&#xA0;&#xA0;&#xA0;&#xA0;&#xA0;&#xA0;</p>";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}}}i:6;a:6:{s:4:"data";s:0:"";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";s:5:"child";a:4:{s:0:"";a:6:{s:5:"title";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:53:"Steve Hackett &amp; Steve Rothery - The Roaring Waves";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:11:"description";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:7:"Steve²";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:4:"link";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:90:"https://www.heavyblogisheavy.com/2026/09/03/steve-hackett-steve-rothery-the-roaring-waves/";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:4:"guid";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:24:"6a91aef0321ef100015dd32b";s:7:"attribs";a:1:{s:0:"";a:1:{s:11:"isPermaLink";s:5:"false";}}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:8:"category";a:5:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:7:"Reviews";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}i:1;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:13:"Steve Hackett";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}i:2;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:13:"Steve Rothery";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}i:3;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:7:"Genesis";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}i:4;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:9:"Marillion";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}s:7:"pubDate";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:29:"Thu, 03 Sep 2026 14:00:27 GMT";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:32:"http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/";a:1:{s:7:"creator";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:17:"Mitchell Connolly";s:7:"attribs";a:0:{}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:29:"http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/";a:1:{s:7:"content";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:0:"";s:7:"attribs";a:1:{s:0:"";a:2:{s:3:"url";s:103:"https://storage.ghost.io/c/8b/c6/8bc603be-b831-44f6-8ffb-773be5bd45c8/content/images/2026/08/images.jpg";s:6:"medium";s:5:"image";}}s:8:"xml_base";s:0:"";s:17:"xml_base_explicit";b:0;s:8:"xml_lang";s:0:"";}}}s:40:"http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/";a:1:{s:7:"encoded";a:1:{i:0;a:5:{s:4:"data";s:4918:"<img src="https://storage.ghost.io/c/8b/c6/8bc603be-b831-44f6-8ffb-773be5bd45c8/content/images/2026/08/images.jpg" alt="Steve Hackett &amp; Steve Rothery - The Roaring Waves"><p>The age-old adage: what do you get when <strong>Genesis</strong>&apos;s Steve Hackett and <strong>Marillion</strong>&apos;s Steve Rothery cross proggy paths? Hopefully you haven&apos;t been asking that question too long, since <em>The Roaring Waves</em> is the first instance of the duo collaborating studio-wise. Yes, Rothery joined Hackett during the latter&apos;s 2013 concert at the Hammersmith Odeon, but this marks the premiere point of studio cooperation. </p><p>It&apos;s quite surprising that these two musicians, each with decades upon decades of expertise in adjacent fields, only released a project last Friday. Hackett&apos;s been silent since 2023&apos;s <em>The Circus and the Nightwhale</em>, and Rothery&apos;s last release, <em>The Ghosts of Pripyat</em>, is nine years <em>Nightwhale&apos;s </em>elder. Eight years in the making led up to this (which explains the dip in activity from the respective players), so it&apos;s about time some classic, guitar-driven prog came my way, and I&apos;d never ignore an artistic couplet such as <strong>Hackett &amp; Rothery</strong>, so follow me as we dive straight into <em>The Roaring Waves</em>. </p><p>It comes as no surprise, considering the kith and kin that are two progressive rock guitarists, that the record is purely instrumental. Think of it comfortably, rather than a full-throttle, blistering shredfest akin to some<em> </em>breakaway <strong>Dream Theater </strong>endeavor. The Steves rely on textural atmospherics instead of masturbatory showiness to craft the record&apos;s concept: the &quot;profundity of the sea&quot;.  Don&apos;t bend your legs and widen your feet just yet&#x2013;the two artists bring the ocean to you. Whether they do so through a steady rocking pattern or electronic-laden passages is up to the natural course of Mother Nature&apos;s vast waterscapes. While the idea is intriguing, Hackett and Rothery only periodically conjure the nautical imagery exhibited on the album&apos;s cover. While the masterful duo trade winding, melancholic notes over shifting drum patterns (see &quot;Sandsend,&quot; &quot;The Black Sea&quot;) or simple ambience (&quot;K-129&quot;), there aren&apos;t enough sonic tinges to communicate such an expansive, oceanic environment. Little nods, like the natural sound effects in the opening track &quot;The Storm,&quot; or the electronic pitter-patter sprinkled throughout <em>The Roaring Waves</em>&apos; bulk of songs, rein me in from deeming the LP a standard instrumental release.</p><p>This brings up a talking point heavily debated within the rock and metal community. Are instrumental records boring? Intrinsically, they are, when considering the heaviness of the genre. Of course, ambient albums surely mustn&apos;t include lyrics, but should progressive rock? Plenty of classics, like <strong>Camel</strong>&apos;s <em>The Snow Goose</em> or <strong>Mike Oldfield</strong>&apos;s myriad of sprawling epics, benefit from the instrumental&apos;s activity. There&apos;s nothing stagnant about these examples, hence their cult fame. <em>The Roaring Waves</em> waits until the final song, &quot;Pacific Coast Highway,&quot; to supply any rocking action, and even that doesn&apos;t honestly fit in with the sea-ness. The twangy rip from Hackett&apos;s harmonica and symphonic feel aren&apos;t choices I would&apos;ve made to muster oceanic vibes, and for this excitement, I had to tread thirty-eight-or-so minutes of consoling yet unwavering waters. I didn&apos;t plunge to the depths of <em>The Roaring Waves</em> expecting a tsunami; more of a point break. Instead, my bark swayed gently, rendering the gumption I gathered unnecessary.</p><p>They can play their water pans and layer keyboard upon synth, but I want more from these illustrious talents. Granted, I wasn&apos;t a huge fan of Marillion (or neo-prog, actually), and Genesis fell off for me, along with everyone else, when the 80s pop wave decimated prog. Hackett helped the band navigate some of the finest recordings of the 70s; however, he should&apos;ve administered a larger dose of his vintage spirit with his guitarmate. Dexterously, this collaboration fell short, but if you need forty-five minutes of simple backing tunes, you&apos;ll find these waves better fit for your boat. </p>
<!--kg-card-begin: html-->
<iframe style="border: 0; width: 100%; height: 120px;" src="https://bandcamp.com/EmbeddedPlayer/album=1536415068/size=large/bgcol=333333/linkcol=e99708/tracklist=false/artwork=small/transparent=true/" seamless><a href="https://insideoutmusic.bandcamp.com/album/the-roaring-waves-24-bit-hd-audio?ref=heavyblogisheavy.com">The Roaring Waves (24-bit HD audio) by Steve Hackett, Steve Rothery</a></iframe>
